[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"121","stub":{"id":"121","section":"03. Lower Oak Creek/ Red Rock Crossing","altname":"Sedona to Red Rock Crossing","river":"Oak Creek","difficulty":"IItoIIIplus","states":["USA-ARZ"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"26","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"109681","location":{"latitude":"34.858002","longitude":"-111.764"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"114934","location":{"latitude":"34.824845","longitude":"-111.808"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"5","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"XXJ8NBvMIA67FMPprhnyg","title":"Wood Strainer","post_date":"2023-03-20 13:53:56+00","detail":"There is a log through the main channel at (34.83265, -111.79495). This should be quite easy to portage on creek left.","user":{"name":"Owen Coutts"}}],"photos":[{"id":"10787","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5331","description":"","caption":"Oak Creek in Sedona-Oak Creek","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10787","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10787"}},{"id":"873283","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873284","description":"near the wall rapid","caption":"Oak Creek Arizona","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873283","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873283"}},{"id":"873225","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873226","description":"This is the view from below the wall rapid with loads of trees in the top pool drop","caption":"near wall rapid","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873225","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873225"}},{"id":"10788","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5331","description":"","caption":"Beautiful Oak Creek ner downtown Sedona","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10788","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10788"}},{"id":"10789","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5331","description":"","caption":"Oak Creek at flood","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10789","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10789"}},{"id":"10791","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5331","description":"","caption":"2004 oak Creek flood","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10791","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10791"}},{"id":"10792","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5331","description":"","caption":"2004 flood at Red Rock crossing","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10792","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10792"}},{"id":"880031","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"881089","description":"This is the first big drop after the Park. The high water two years ago cleaned out the drop quite a bit and has made it much easier for boats (especially tandems) to get down","caption":"oak creek at park","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/880031","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/880031"}}],"detail":{"id":"121","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This is a scenic run and a good introduction to Oak Creek Canyon and should be pounced on when elusive water levels are available by boaters with advanced to expert skills. Use a variety of high elevation put-ins for additional mank value.\n\nIn this run there are at least four named rapid features with many relatively long wave trains and little drops amongs relatively short pools. This creek has a narrow and braided channel and is woody along its entire length. The potential for strainers and log jams to develop as a result of higher flows and falling trees is ever present. Always scout when in doubt. Private concrete driveways with culverts present lowhead dam and entrapment hazards at a range of flows.\n\nThe 80 FPM gradient makes this run an exhilerating hoot for a solid Class III boater at 500 CFS or less. Class IV and V boaters have run this section at over 10,000 CFS with various results. Heavily treed rock and boulder bar pour-overs acting as giant combs await boaters unable to see or make the tight turns into the main channel. When Oak Creek gets over 500 CFS, treat it like a Class IV run and scout frequently.\n\nThis run also has plenty of surf potential along the sandstone chutes.\n\nTake out in Sedona at the 179 bridge or go further about five miles downstream on river left to take out at Red Rock Crossing. Use Verde Valley School Rd parking. From the 179 in Village of Oak Creek turn West on Verde Valley School Rd and drive 5 miles to the end of the road. There is a short walk down to the creek.\n\nThere is also another option to takeout one mile south in Red Rock State Park on river right. off of highway 89A.\n\nFor additional information refer to \"Paddling Arizona\" by Tyler Williams.","averageGradient":"48","maxGradient":"0","length":"4.3","correlations":[{"reachID":"121","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetai